+  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
+    // VOYAGER: compact form on a landscape screen.
+    //
+    // The full-width bar is right on a phone: three labelled targets across the
+    // bottom, framed like the driving panels. On a dashboard in landscape the
+    // same bar is an opaque band roughly a fifth of the screen high, taken out
+    // of the middle of the driver's view of the map — and Voyager already
+    // carries its own controls, so the labels are explaining a second set of
+    // buttons next to the first.
+    //
+    // Compact keeps all three destinations, drops the labels and the band, and
+    // sits in the corner. It also stops honouring bottomInset, which the host
+    // app inflates to lift the floating map controls clear of its own chrome:
+    // useful for a button that floats, wrong for a bar that would grow by the
+    // same amount.
+    // VOYAGER: always compact, in every orientation.
+    //
+    // This used to switch on orientation — full width in portrait, compact in
+    // landscape — because portrait originally docked Voyager's own chrome in a
+    // Column below a constrained map area, so this bar had its own untouched
+    // strip to sit in. Voyager's dashboard now floats its chrome over a
+    // full-bleed map in both orientations, which means this bar and that
+    // chrome occupy the same physical screen everywhere, not only in
+    // landscape. Compact is the only form that leaves room for both.
+    return _buildCompact(context);
